<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iberians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iberians were the pre-Roman peoples of the eastern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, among them the Turdetani, Edetani and Ilergetes. They spoke a non-Indo-European language written in their own scripts, lived in fortified towns and produced sculpture such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche">Lady of Elche</a>. Contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> shaped their culture before Roman conquest from the late 3rd century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iberians.jpg" alt="Iberians" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heparina1985 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_of_Elche_at_the_National_Arquaeological_Museum,_Madrid_(Spain).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Greek and Roman writers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, describe the Iberians, and Roman sources also call them Hispani. The ancient authors used the name in two senses: generally, for all the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, and in the narrower ethnic sense used here, for the people of the eastern and southern coasts. By the 6th century BCE they had absorbed influences from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><p>They lived in villages and oppida, or fortified settlements, in tribal communities, and those in the Spanish Levant were more urbanised than the peoples of the centre and northwest, who spoke Celtic dialects. In the late 5th and early 4th centuries BCE an aristocracy consolidated, and the oppidum became the political centre of the landscape.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Iberians traded widely, and their pottery and metalwork have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. They had extensive contact with Greek colonists at Emporion and with Phoenician colonies in the south such as Gadir. Sculptures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-baza">Lady of Baza</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche">Lady of Elche</a> are thought to be the work of Iberians fairly well acquainted with Greek art. From the 5th century BCE Iberian soldiers were often sent to fight in Italy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> expanded in the peninsula under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, who besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a> and so began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. Many Iberian warriors fought on both sides, though most tribes sided with Carthage. Rome sent Gnaeus and Publius Cornelius Scipio, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> arrived in 210 BCE it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> and won the Battle of Ilipa. The conquered lands became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, and fighting in the north continued until 16 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Iberian society had kings or chieftains, nobles, priests, artisans and slaves; the nobles met in a council that ancient sources call a senate. The Iberians adopted wine and olives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, bred horses, mined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, and made iron weapons such as the falcata. They produced sculpture in stone and bronze, and their religion was polytheistic and influenced by Greek, Carthaginian and Phoenician practice. They cremated their dead. They wrote in three scripts, and their language, unclassified and non-Indo-European, became extinct by the 1st or 2nd century CE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> replaced it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iberians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 36 BCE Publius Canidius Crassus, a general of Mark Antony, campaigned against the <b>Iberians</b> and Albanians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ancient writers explained their name as a mixture of Celts and <b>Iberians</b>, but modern scholars treat it as a geographical and cultural label coined by Greek and Roman observers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1282 John II married Eudokia, daughter of Michael VIII Palaiologos, and adopted a more modest title, Emperor and Autocrat of all the East, the <b>Iberians</b> and the Perateia, the last term referring to dependencies across the Black Sea in the Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…BCE, Media, Caucasian Iberia and the Seleucids had seized the kingdom's remote regions; Strabo records how Artaxias campaigned east to reunite Caspiane and Paytakaran, north to defeat the <b>Iberians</b> and regain Gugark, west to recover Karin, Ekeghik and Derjan, and south, after many battles with the Seleucids, Tmorik.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south-west the Bagratid princes of Tao-Klarjeti revived the title of king of the <b>Iberians</b> in 888, while in the west the Kingdom of Abkhazia, ruled from Kutaisi, had become a Georgian-speaking Orthodox realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 888 his descendant Adarnase IV took the title king of the <b>Iberians</b>, restoring the royal title in Georgia after three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-iberians">Kingdom of the Iberians</a></li></ul></section>
